      "title": "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors by Identifying Pivot Columns"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "93453d85-9925-59aa-a97f-e85c46233e89",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}_{1} &=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\qquad \\mathbf{a}_{2} &=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf{a}_{3} &=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind a basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $S=\\operatorname{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_{1},\\mathbf{a}_{2},\\mathbf{a}_{3}\\}$ by identifying pivot columns.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Place the vectors in the columns of a matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis matrix is already in row echelon form.\n\nThe leading nonzero entries are in columns $1, 2$. Select the corresponding columns from the **original matrix**, giving $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{a}_{1},\\;\\mathbf{a}_{2}\\}$:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\;\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThese pivot columns are linearly independent and span the column space of $A$, which is $S$. The nonpivot columns can be removed without changing the span. Row operations locate the pivot indices; use the original vectors when writing the basis.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
